%I A398173 #26 Aug 09 2026 17:27:58
%S A398173 3,4,5,7,7,8,9,10,11,11,12,13,13,13,14,15,15,16,16,16
%N A398173 a(n) is the minimum size of a subset of Z/pZ with at least 2 elements and no unique sum, where p is the n-th odd prime.
%C A398173 For a subset A of Z/pZ, an element g of A + A is called a unique sum of A if there is exactly one pair {a, b} of elements of A, a = b allowed, with a + b = g, and A has no unique sum if no element of A + A is a unique sum. For odd p this is equivalent to every element of A + A having at least 3 representations as an ordered sum a + b with a, b in A: a single pair {a, b} yields one ordered sum when a = b and two when a != b, and two different pairs {a, a}, {b, b} cannot yield the same sum since doubling is injective in Z/pZ for odd p. The minimum in the name is denoted m(p) in the literature.
%C A398173 Ben Green's "100 Open Problems" includes the problem of estimating m(p) as Problem 27 and credits the question to Andrew Granville and Swastik Kopparty.
%C A398173 The odd primes are the natural index set: in Z/2Z the only subset with at least 2 elements is {0, 1}, where 0 + 1 = 1 has just 2 ordered representations, so no such subset exists for p = 2.
%H A398173 B. Bedert, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00493-023-00069-w">On unique sums in Abelian groups</a>, Combinatorica, 44 (2024), 269-298.
%H A398173 Ben Green, <a href="https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/greenbj/papers/open-problems.pdf">100 Open Problems</a> (see Problem 27).
%H A398173 T. Łuczak and T. Schoen, <a href="https://www.impan.pl/en/publishing-house/journals-and-series/acta-arithmetica/all/134/2/83513/on-a-problem-of-konyagin">On a problem of Konyagin</a>, Acta Arith., 134 (2008), 101-109.
%H A398173 E. G. Straus, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-314X(76)90019-6">Differences of residues (mod p)</a>, J. Number Theory, 8 (1976), 40-42.
%H A398173 Mark Watson, <a href="https://github.com/mkwatson/unique-sums-notes">Unique sums notes</a> (notes on Bedert's paper, with a machine-checked proposition).
%F A398173 a(n) >= w(p)*log(p) for some function w(p) tending to infinity with p, where p is the n-th odd prime; proved by Bedert (2024).
%F A398173 a(n) <= C*(log(p))^2 for some constant C; proved by Bedert (2024), and also follows from Łuczak and Schoen (2008), Theorem 2, together with Straus (1976).
%e A398173 a(2) = 4: for p = 5 the set {0, 1, 2, 3} works, since the ordered representation counts of the sums 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 (mod 5) are 3, 3, 3, 4, 3 respectively, e.g., 3 = 0+3 = 1+2 = 2+1 = 3+0; so a(2) <= 4. Checking all ten 3-element subsets of Z/5Z shows none works, so a(2) = 4.
%e A398173 a(15) = 14: {0, 1, 5, 7, 14, 16, 18, 28, 32, 35, 36, 39, 43, 51}.
%e A398173 a(16) = 15: {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 9, 10, 16, 25, 27, 32, 42, 44, 48}.
%e A398173 a(17) = 15: {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 15, 21, 22, 24, 42, 49, 55, 56, 58}.
%e A398173 a(18) = 16: {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 11, 14, 15, 25, 26, 50, 53, 54}.
%e A398173 a(19) = 16: {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 12, 31, 45, 46, 57, 59, 61, 64}.
%e A398173 a(20) = 16: {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9, 13, 21, 23, 25, 29, 44, 45, 65}.
%o A398173 (Python)
%o A398173 # Exhaustive check from the definition; feasible through about p = 13.
%o A398173 from itertools import combinations
%o A398173 def m(p):
%o A398173     for k in range(2, p + 1):
%o A398173         for A in combinations(range(p), k):
%o A398173             c = [0] * p
%o A398173             for a in A:
%o A398173                 for b in A:
%o A398173                     c[(a + b) % p] += 1
%o A398173             if all(v == 0 or v >= 3 for v in c):
%o A398173                 return k
%o A398173 print([m(p) for p in (3, 5, 7, 11, 13)])
%Y A398173 Cf. A065091 (the odd primes), A211316 (maximum size of a sum-free subset of Z/nZ), A392461, A396704 (extremal sets with bounded representation multiplicities).
